  "tables": [
    {
      "caption": "",
      "index": 0,
      "text": "As you work through the lab: Q#: Bold, numbered items reference things that need to be included in your lab report questions to answer, data, plots, or drawings ■ Items with a square bullet are action items you’ll need to do to progress through the lab. ➤ Items with an arrow are hints, tips, or things to keep in mind when completing tasks. Boxed information is about either the expectations for your lab reports or key concepts for the course."
    },
    {
      "caption": "",
      "index": 1,
      "text": "What is a Model? An equation and its assumptions are a model—a representation of physical behavior that may be expected to apply to a real system under certain conditions as stated. A model should both explain and predict physical behavior. Experiments are designed to check our understanding by testing a prediction made using the model, or hypothesis, of the situation. Start by using a hypothesis, or model, to make a prediction about the expected behavior of a specific system. (In this activity the hypothesis is provided.) Design an experiment to check this prediction. The experiment should be designed such that the data (and subsequent analysis) can either agree or disagree with prediction. If your results agree with your prediction, then we say that the experiment supports the hypothesis or model. If not, we say that the experiment refutes the hypothesis. All models have limitations in their applicability; experiments are the means by which we explore those limits."
    },
    {
      "caption": "",
      "index": 2,
      "text": "Assumptions A hypothesis typically includes assumptions about the situation being modeled. This could be ignoring a force or assuming it’s constant, or making some other simplification. For example: In free fall, we assume air resistance is negligible (so small that its effects can be ignored). Hooke’s law assumes the elasticity of a spring is constant – the spring constant. Calculations of Earth’s gravity may assume a perfectly spherical planet. These assumptions may or may not be justified. For example: A billiard ball on Earth falls for at least short distances with an acceleration of g, the same as in a vacuum, as if there were no air. Assuming air resistance is negligible is valid when considering its motion. A sheet of paper falls much more slowly. The assumption that air resistance is negligible is not valid – predictions made from a model that ignores air resistance will not match what is observed. Identifying, justifying, and checking assumptions is an important part of an experiment."
    }
  ],
